from scriptkit import sc, ch, save, S

# Grandma Ruth 112 · ANGLE: humiliated grandma + reveal (rich in-laws mock a plain gift; it's the deed to the family farm)

sc("""Sweetheart, Christmas of two thousand twenty-two, my friend Dottie walked into a mansion on Lake Erie with a little box wrapped in brown paper and kitchen twine. A gift for her granddaughter and her new husband. And in front of forty guests, the groom's mother held it up and said, oh, how sweet, did you wrap it in a grocery bag? Everybody laughed. Two hours later, nobody in that room was laughing.""",
   "small box wrapped in brown paper and twine", "A GROCERY BAG?")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you what was inside that little box, why Dottie had wrapped it so plain on purpose, and the letter at the bottom that made the groom stand up in front of his whole family and say something to his mother he'd never said before. Stay with me, honey. Dottie told me every word.""",
   "mansion living room Christmas party guests", "WHAT WAS INSIDE")

ch("Dottie")
sc("""Dottie and I have been friends for sixty years, honey. She and her husband Ray had a farm south of Mansfield, a hundred and twenty acres of corn and soybeans and an old white farmhouse with a wraparound porch. Ray's grandfather built it in nineteen ten. They raised three children there. Ray passed in two thousand nineteen, and Dottie stayed on, by herself, with the barn cats and a lot of memories.""",
   "old white farmhouse with wraparound porch", "120 ACRES")
sc("""Her granddaughter Emily practically grew up on that farm, sweetheart. Every summer, she was there, riding on the tractor with Ray, collecting eggs, falling asleep on the porch swing. When Emily was little, she used to say, Grandma, when I grow up, I'm going to live here forever. And Dottie would laugh and say, we'll see, sweet pea. We'll see. In a minute I'll tell you why that little promise mattered.""",
   "little girl on porch swing farmhouse summer", "LIVE HERE FOREVER")
sc("""Emily grew up and became a nurse, honey, and in twenty twenty-two, she married a young man named Josh Whitmore. A good boy, quiet, an engineer. But his family had money. Real money. His father owned car dealerships all over northern Ohio, and his mother, Caroline, had a house on Lake Erie with eleven bathrooms. Dottie counted them. And from the very first day, Caroline made it clear Emily wasn't good enough.""",
   "large lakefront mansion in winter", "ELEVEN BATHROOMS")

ch("Caroline")
sc("""Little things, sweetheart. At the engagement dinner, Caroline asked Emily what her father did, and when Emily said he drove a truck, Caroline said, oh, how honest. At the wedding, she seated Dottie at the table by the kitchen door. And she called Emily, quote, Josh's little nurse. Not Emily. Josh's little nurse. Dottie heard it with her own ears, and she didn't say a word. Not yet.""",
   "wedding reception table near kitchen door", "JOSH'S LITTLE NURSE")
sc("""And Dottie noticed something else, honey. Emily and Josh were living in an apartment over one of Josh's father's dealerships. Rent free. But Caroline had a key, and she came in whenever she liked. She rearranged Emily's kitchen. She threw out Emily's curtains. Emily called Dottie crying one night and said, Grandma, I don't feel like I have a home. Dottie hung up the phone and sat in Ray's chair for a long time.""",
   "young woman crying on phone in apartment kitchen", "I DON'T HAVE A HOME")

sc("""Dottie called me that week, honey, and asked me to come out to the farm. We sat at her kitchen table with the old oilcloth, and she said, Ruth, what would you do? And I said, Dottie, I'd stand up at that Christmas party and give that woman a piece of my mind. And Dottie laughed and said, that's why I'm asking you, and then doing the opposite. I'll tell you what she did instead in a minute.""",
   "two elderly women at farmhouse kitchen table", "DOING THE OPPOSITE")
ch("The party")
sc("""If you're enjoying Grandma's story, tap the like button for me, sweetheart. It helps more people find these stories. The Whitmores' Christmas party was a big affair, honey. Forty guests, a man in a tuxedo playing a white piano, a tree that touched the ceiling, and presents stacked up underneath in silver paper from the department stores. Dottie came in her church dress and her good wool coat, with Ray's old watch on a ribbon around her neck.""",
   "white grand piano by huge Christmas tree", "SILVER PAPER")
sc("""She'd wrapped her gift in brown paper from a feed sack, tied with kitchen twine and a sprig of cedar from the farm. On purpose, honey. She told me later, I wanted it to look like what it was. A piece of the farm. And when she set it under that tree, among all that silver paper, Caroline picked it right back up and held it high for the whole room to see.""",
   "brown paper gift among silver presents under tree", "A PIECE OF THE FARM")
sc("""And she said, in that bright, loud voice, oh, Dorothy, how sweet. Did you wrap it in a grocery bag? And forty people laughed, sweetheart. The man at the piano stopped playing. And Caroline put Dottie's present on the side table, with the children's gifts and the dog's Christmas stocking. And Emily's face went bright red. In a minute I'll tell you what Dottie said back.""",
   "woman holding up brown paper gift laughing", "FORTY PEOPLE LAUGHED")

ch("Dottie's answer")
sc("""Dottie didn't get angry, honey. She'd been a farm wife for fifty years. You don't get angry at hail. You just wait it out. She smiled and said, well, Caroline, it's what was handy. And she sat down in the corner with a cup of punch. But Josh had seen it. And Josh had seen his wife's face. And he didn't say anything. Not yet. He just went very quiet.""",
   "elderly woman sitting calmly with punch cup", "WAIT OUT THE HAIL")
sc("""So far, a farm built in nineteen ten, a little girl's promise, a mother-in-law with eleven bathrooms, and a brown paper box next to the dog's stocking. When the gifts were opened, late in the evening, everybody gathered around. Josh's father gave them a set of golf clubs. Caroline gave them a crystal punch bowl. All very fancy. Everyone clapped. And then Emily picked up the brown paper box. Stay with me, honey.""",
   "young couple opening gifts in fancy living room", "THE BROWN PAPER BOX")

sc("""Caroline said, in that same bright voice, oh, look, Grandma's present. Let's see what the farm sent. And a few people chuckled again. Emily looked over at her grandmother in the corner. And Dottie just nodded at her, once, the way she used to nod at Emily on the tractor when it was time to let out the clutch. Go ahead, sweet pea. And Emily's hands were shaking a little as she pulled at the twine.""",
   "young woman untying twine on small box", "GO AHEAD, SWEET PEA")
ch("Inside the box")
sc("""She untied the twine and peeled back the paper. Inside was an old cigar box of Ray's. And inside that was an old brass key, on a loop of red yarn. Emily knew that key, sweetheart. It was the key to the front door of the farmhouse, the one Ray's grandfather had made. She'd used it a thousand times. And under the key, folded in thirds, was a stack of papers with a county seal.""",
   "old brass key on red yarn in cigar box", "THE FARMHOUSE KEY")
sc("""It was a deed, honey. Dottie had gone to her lawyer in October. She'd put the farmhouse and twenty acres around it, the house, the barn, the orchard, the porch swing, in Emily and Josh's names. And kept the rest of the land in her own name, to keep farming with her neighbor's boys. The room went silent. Caroline's punch bowl was still sitting there in its silver paper.""",
   "property deed with county seal on table", "IN THEIR NAMES")
sc("""Somebody near the piano, a man who sold real estate, said quietly, that's Route Thirteen land. That's worth more than this house. But that wasn't the part that mattered, sweetheart. The part that mattered was at the very bottom of the cigar box. A letter, in Dottie's handwriting, on lined paper. And Emily read it out loud, because she couldn't help it, standing in the middle of that room.""",
   "handwritten letter on lined paper", "AT THE BOTTOM")

ch("The letter")
sc("""It said, Emily, sweet pea. When you were six, you told me you'd live here forever. I'm keeping my end. Every young wife needs one place in this world where nobody else has a key. This is yours. No one can rearrange your kitchen here. No one can throw out your curtains. Grandpa Ray would want you on that porch swing. Come home whenever you like, and stay as long as you want. Love, Grandma.""",
   "young woman reading letter with tears", "NOBODY ELSE HAS A KEY")
sc("""Emily got to the part about the curtains, honey, and she couldn't read anymore. She just stood there and cried. And everybody in that room understood exactly what Dottie meant, because everybody in that room had seen Caroline throw those curtains out, or heard about it. Caroline went white. She opened her mouth. And her son, quiet, gentle Josh, stood up before she could say a word.""",
   "young man standing up in crowded room", "JOSH STOOD UP")

ch("Josh")
sc("""He said, Mom. You laughed at Grandma Dottie's present in front of forty people. You called my wife my little nurse at our own wedding. You've let yourself into our home for six months. And I let you, because I didn't want a fight. That's on me. Then he took Emily's hand and said, it stops tonight. We're going home. And then he looked at the key and said, our home.""",
   "young man holding wife's hand speaking firmly", "IT STOPS TONIGHT")
sc("""And he went over to Dottie, sweetheart, in her corner with her punch, and he knelt down in front of her, in his good suit, and he said, Mrs. Dottie, thank you. I'm sorry I didn't say something sooner. And Dottie patted his cheek and said, you said it when it counted, son. Ray always said a man's measured by what he does when it counts. They left the party together, the three of them, before dessert.""",
   "young man kneeling before elderly woman", "WHEN IT COUNTED")

sc("""Dottie told me that on the drive home, honey, nobody said anything for twenty minutes. Then Emily, in the back seat, started to laugh. And Josh started to laugh. And Dottie started to laugh. And they laughed all the way down Route Thirteen in the snow, until Dottie had to pull over, because she couldn't see the road through her tears. Josh said, Grandma Dottie, did you just give us a farm in a feed sack?""",
   "car driving on snowy country road at night", "A FARM IN FEED SACK")
ch("Spring")
sc("""Emily and Josh moved into that farmhouse in March, honey. Josh fixed the porch roof himself. Emily painted the kitchen yellow and hung curtains she sewed from Dottie's old flour sack fabric. And Dottie moved into the little apartment over the garage, so she could have her coffee on the porch every morning with her granddaughter. Last summer, they had a baby girl. They named her Raye, after Grandpa.""",
   "young family on farmhouse porch with baby", "THEY NAMED HER RAYE")
sc("""And Caroline? It took her almost a year, sweetheart. But last Christmas, she drove down from the lake, and she knocked on that farmhouse door. She knocked, honey. She didn't have a key. And when Emily opened it, Caroline was holding a box wrapped in plain brown paper and kitchen twine. Inside was a baby quilt. And a note that said, I'm sorry. I'm learning.""",
   "woman holding brown paper gift at farmhouse door", "SHE KNOCKED")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, honey. Don't judge a gift by its paper. And don't judge a grandmother by her coat. Tell me in the comments, sweetheart, what's the most precious gift you ever got that came in plain wrapping? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen. Good night. Merry Christmas.""",
   "brass key on red yarn by Christmas tree", "DON'T JUDGE THE PAPER")

for s in S:
    s["cuts"], s["moves"] = 4, True

